course: dictionary
lesson: 14487
title: Spotting a patronising tone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show their meaning in how they are said. Patronising describes someone who talks to you as if you were less clever or less important than they are. The words can sound friendly, but they show no real respect. «Oh, well done, sweetie» sounds nice, but said to a grown-up who has just solved a hard puzzle, it would be patronising.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Who is speaking, how are they speaking, and does the listener seem small or annoyed? «You are doing really well for someone your age» sounds kind, but the words "for someone your age" show that the speaker expects very little from you. That is what makes it patronising.
explain: Patronising is an adjective, so it describes a voice, a smile, a tone or a character. Writers use it to show a character who looks down on others. Test your meaning by swapping in a simpler phrase: "talking down to someone" fits the sentence, so patronising is a good match.
fact: Patronising means talking to someone as if they were less clever or less important than you.
fact: A patronising tone seems kind on the surface but shows no respect.
fact: «Oh, well done, sweetie» said to a grown person can sound patronising.
fact: In US spelling the word is patronizing, with a z.
fact: In a more formal sense, patronise is a verb meaning to be a regular customer of a shop or business.
exercise: «The teacher's patronising smile made Sam feel very small.» What does patronising describe here? | *Talking down to someone | Happy and excited | Loud and noisy | Wet and cold
exercise: Jayden's uncle says, «Ah, bless you, you're so clever for a little one!» Why might Jayden feel annoyed? | The uncle is asking him to sing | He is being given a present | *The uncle thinks he is less clever than he really is | He is being told a joke
exercise: Which word means talking down to someone in a kind-seeming but rude way? | Curious | Generous | *Patronising | Cheerful
test: A waiter says, «Aren't you a clever girl for ordering by yourself?» What is patronising about it? | *It treats her as if being capable is surprising, as if she is less able than she is | It is written in capital letters | It is a question about the weather | It offers her a free drink
test: Which sentence shows a patronising tone? | «I like your red coat.» | «Could you pass the salt, please?» | «The bus leaves at nine.» | *«Here, let me explain it again, slowly, in case you miss the point.»
exam: Read this: «Good girl, you did it, what a clever little helper.» Which best describes the tone? | Cruel and angry | Sad and quiet | *Patronising, because it treats the helper as less capable than an adult | Friendly and respectful, showing real pride
